    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-117602",
      "knowledge_point_id": "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function",
      "problem": "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is any integer.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
