      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer.",
