    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-117612",
      "knowledge_point_id": "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460",
      "knowledge_point": "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =3\\pi +6n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=3\\pi +6n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.",
