    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-117618",
      "knowledge_point_id": "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225",
      "knowledge_point": "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=(2n+1)\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =(2n+1)\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =2n\\pi +\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.",
