      "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=(2n+1)\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =(2n+1)\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\pi +2\\pi n \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n,$ where $n$ is an integer.",
