    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65937",
      "knowledge_point_id": "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225",
      "knowledge_point": "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x-3\\pi }{2}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x-3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x-3\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2\\pi +4n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=2\\pi +4n\\pi ,$  where $n$ is an integer.",
