      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function",
      "problem": "Find a general expression describing all of the zeros of $y = \\sin\\left( \\dfrac x 2\\right).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX =  \\dfrac x 2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=\\sin X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX =n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x =2n\\pi,$ where $n$ is any integer.",
